---
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro'
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro'
import Head from '../components/Head.astro'
import { allMarkets, readMarket } from '../lib/markets.js'
import '../styles/global.css'
const markets = allMarkets()
const sc = markets.map((m) => readMarket(m.tag).sourceCounts ?? {})
  .reduce((a, s) => ({ maps: a.maps + (s.maps ?? 0), organic: a.organic + (s.organic ?? 0), both: a.both + (s.both ?? 0) }),
          { maps: 0, organic: 0, both: 0 })
const overlapPct = Math.round((sc.both / (sc.maps + sc.organic - sc.both)) * 100)

const SECTIONS = [
  { id: 'companies', n: '01', t: 'Which companies count' },
  { id: 'depth',     n: '02', t: 'How deep we sample' },
  { id: 'terms',     n: '03', t: 'Which search terms' },
  { id: 'cities',    n: '04', t: 'Two cities, no suburbs' },
  { id: 'filtering', n: '05', t: 'What is not a local business' },
  { id: 'scores',    n: '06', t: 'What the scores actually are' },
  { id: 'claims',    n: '07', t: 'What we do not claim' },
]

const TITLE = 'Methodology: how the DFW link graph is built · DFW SEO'
const DESCRIPTION =
  'How the DFW link graph is built: which companies we count, how deep we sample, what the scores mean, and what we deliberately do not claim.'

/*
 * No Dataset here: this page is the prose describing how the datasets are
 * built, not a dataset itself. `about` points at the thing it documents, which
 * is the honest relationship and the one that helps a crawler understand why
 * every market page links back here.
 */
const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'Methodology',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    url: new URL('/methodology/', Astro.site).href,
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'DFW SEO', url: new URL('/', Astro.site).href },
    about: { '@type': 'Dataset', name: 'The DFW link graph', url: new URL('/', Astro.site).href },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'DFW link graph', item: new URL('/', Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Methodology' },
    ],
  },
]
---
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <Head title={TITLE} description={DESCRIPTION} card="methodology" schema={schema} />
</head>
<body>
  <Nav />

  <header class="head wrap">
    <div class="head-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
      <h1>How this was built</h1>
      <p class="lede">Every choice below was measured rather than assumed, and each one records
      the number that drove it. Where a method has a limit, it is stated here rather than left
      for you to find.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="doc wrap">
    <nav class="toc">
      <p class="toc-h">Contents</p>
      {SECTIONS.map((s) => <a href={`#${s.id}`}><span class="mono">{s.n}</span>{' '}{s.t}</a>)}
    </nav>

    <article class="body">
      <section id="companies">
        <p class="num mono">01</p>
        <h2>Which companies count</h2>
        <p>Google gives two different answers to "who are the X companies in this city", and
        we use both.</p>
        <div class="cards">
          <div class="card">
            <span class="src" data-s="maps">map pack</span>
            <p>A verified Google Business Profile near the city center. Definitively a real
            local business.</p>
            <p class="caveat"><b>Limit:</b> proximity-weighted. It reflects businesses near
            the centroid, not the whole city. A search from Highland Park returns a different
            set.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="src" data-s="organic">organic</span>
            <p>Ranks in the unqualified organic results. Authority-weighted rather than
            proximity-weighted, so it covers ground the map pack misses.</p>
            <p class="caveat"><b>Limit:</b> pulls in directories, national chains and
            manufacturers that have to be filtered out.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card is-both">
            <span class="src" data-s="both">both</span>
            <p>Locally real <em>and</em> organically competitive.</p>
            <p class="caveat"><b>The strongest signal in the data</b>, and the rarest.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>Their weaknesses are opposite, which is why we keep and label both rather than
        picking one.</p>
        <figure class="stat">
          <b>{overlapPct}%</b>
          <figcaption>of the businesses we sampled appear at the top of both lists.
          In dentistry the overlap is zero, in both cities.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="depth">
        <p class="num mono">02</p>
        <h2>How deep we sample</h2>
        <p>We take the top 20 map pack results and the top 40 organic results per market, then
        keep up to 12 companies from the merged set.</p>
        <div class="note">
          <p><b>Both lists run deeper than that.</b> Google will paginate much further and we
          do not follow it. Any count on this site describes what we sampled, never the size
          of the market.</p>
        </div>
        <p>We sample the top because that is where the competition is. A business on page four
        of the organic results is not shaping the link economy of its market.</p>
        <p>Across all {markets.length} markets we examined <b class="mono">{sc.maps}</b>{' '}
        businesses from map pack samples and <b class="mono">{sc.organic}</b> from organic
        samples. Only <b class="mono">{sc.both}</b> appeared in both.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="terms">
        <p class="num mono">03</p>
        <h2>Which search terms</h2>
        <p>We search <span class="mono">"roofing contractor"</span> localized to a city, not{' '}
        <span class="mono">"dallas roofing contractor"</span>. Nobody in Dallas types their own
        city name.</p>
        <figure class="stat">
          <b>3/10</b>
          <figcaption>companies shared between the qualified and unqualified versions of the
          same search. The choice changes seven of ten.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Candidate terms are tested against live search volume per city and the highest is
        kept, because phrasing dominance varies. "Plumber" and "plumbing company" are not
        interchangeable, and the winner differs between Dallas and Fort Worth.</p>
        <div class="note">
          <p><b>The search term is a proxy for the market, not a recommendation.</b> We use it
          to find who currently holds authority. We are not suggesting anyone target it. The
          link graph is the subject; the keyword is only how we find the participants.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="cities">
        <p class="num mono">04</p>
        <h2>Two cities, no suburbs</h2>
        <p>Running the same query in each city returns almost entirely different companies.</p>
        <figure class="stat">
          <b>2/10</b>
          <figcaption>companies shared between Dallas and Fort Worth, and one of those two is
          a national publisher. They are two link markets, not one metro.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Suburbs are deliberately excluded. DFW companies serve the whole metro, so
        neighborhood pages would largely duplicate each other.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="filtering">
        <p class="num mono">05</p>
        <h2>What is not a local business</h2>
        <p>A national platform ranks for millions of keywords; a local contractor ranks for a
        few hundred. Anything above 10,000 national keywords is treated as a platform, as is
        any <span class="mono">.gov</span> or <span class="mono">.edu</span> domain.</p>
        <table class="cmp">
          <thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th class="num">National keywords</th><th>Actually</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="mono">yelp.com</td><td class="num mono">31,325,747</td><td>platform</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">homeadvisor.com</td><td class="num mono">641,245</td><td>platform</td></tr>
            <tr class="edge"><td class="mono">muvr.io</td><td class="num mono">9,687</td><td>national marketplace</td></tr>
            <tr class="edge"><td class="mono">dunhamlaw.com</td><td class="num mono">8,867</td><td>real local firm</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">bertroofing.com</td><td class="num mono">969</td><td>real local firm</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Those middle two rows are the honest limit of the test: it cannot separate a
        national chain or a vertical directory from a genuine local firm, because they occupy
        the same range. A short reviewed exclusion list handles that residue. Some judgment is
        irreducible; bounding it and saying so beats a threshold that pretends to work.</p>
        <div class="note">
          <p><b>Platforms are excluded from the company list only.</b> They stay in the graph
          as referring domains, which is where several of them are most interesting.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="scores">
        <p class="num mono">06</p>
        <h2>What the scores actually are</h2>
        <p>This is the part most link studies gloss over, so we will be blunt about it.</p>
        <p><b>Rank and spam score are one vendor's estimates.</b> Rank is a 0 to 1000
        logarithmic authority score. Spam score is a 0 to 100 risk estimate. Neither comes from
        Google. Run the same domains through a different tool and you will get different
        numbers, because every provider models this differently from a different crawl.</p>
        <p>So when we say a domain "scores zero", that is a statement about the metric, not a
        verdict on the link. It does <em>not</em> establish that:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Google assigns it no value. Nobody outside Google can see what Google counts.</li>
          <li>It has no effect. Nofollow links, unlinked brand mentions and plain citations can
          carry weight that no backlink index scores, and increasingly they feed systems that
          are not classical search at all.</li>
          <li>Another tool would agree. They routinely do not.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="note is-key">
          <p>When a large share of a market's shared links score zero, the useful reading is
          not "these are worthless" but <b>"these are held by nearly everyone here, so they are
          not what separates these companies from each other."</b></p>
        </div>
        <p>The one number on this site that is not an estimate is <b>reach</b>: how many of the
        companies a domain links to. That is a direct count, and it is why the connector tables
        are sorted by it.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="claims">
        <p class="num mono">07</p>
        <h2>What we do not claim</h2>
        <p>Every figure describes what is publicly observable in link data. None of it is a
        claim about any company's intent. Where the data shows an unusual pattern, we describe
        the pattern and let you draw the conclusion.</p>
        <div class="note">
          <p>The code that produces all of this is public:{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/braginteractive/dfwseo">github.com/braginteractive/dfwseo</a>.
          Every decision above is documented there with the measurement behind it, including
          the ones that did not work.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
  <Footer />
</body>
</html>

<style>
  /* Same 210px + gap offset as the doc grid so the heading starts on the body column. */
  .head { padding: 60px var(--gutter) 38px; }
  .head-inner { width: min(66ch, 100%); margin: 0 auto; transform: translateX(137px); }
  .head .lede { max-width: 56ch; }
  @media (max-width: 1100px) { .head-inner { transform: none; } }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .head { padding-top: 38px; } }

  /* A reading page wants a measure, not the full page width. The nav still spans
     --page; this column centers inside it. */
  .doc { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 210px minmax(0, 66ch); gap: 64px;
    justify-content: center; align-items: start; padding-bottom: 100px; }

  .toc { position: sticky; top: calc(var(--nav-h) + 24px); padding-top: 6px; }
  .toc-h { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--dim); margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .toc a { display: flex; gap: 10px; align-items: baseline; text-decoration: none; color: var(--dim);
    font-size: 13.5px; padding: 5px 0; line-height: 1.3; }
  .toc a:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  .toc a span { color: #4a5260; font-size: 10.5px; }

  .body { min-width: 0; }
  .body section { padding: 0 0 52px; }
  .body section + section { padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-soft); padding-top: 44px; }
  .num { color: var(--ember); font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: .14em; margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .body h2 { margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .body p { color: var(--ink-soft); text-wrap: pretty; }
  .body b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 500; }
  .body em { color: var(--ink); }
  .body .mono { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .9em; color: var(--ink); }
  .body ul { color: var(--ink-soft); padding-left: 18px; margin: 14px 0 18px; }
  .body li { margin-bottom: 9px; text-wrap: pretty; }

  .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin: 22px 0; }
  .card { background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; }
  .card.is-both { border-color: #2c4536; }
  .card p { font-size: 13.5px; margin: 10px 0 0; line-height: 1.5; }
  .card .caveat { color: var(--dim); font-size: 12.5px; padding-top: 9px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-soft); margin-top: 11px; }

  .src { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; padding: 3px 8px; border-radius: 4px; white-space: nowrap; }
  .src[data-s="both"]    { background: #1d2a22; color: var(--green); }
  .src[data-s="maps"]    { background: #241a13; color: var(--ember); }
  .src[data-s="organic"] { background: #1b2430; color: #6d9dc9; }

  .stat { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 20px; margin: 26px 0; padding: 20px 22px;
    background: var(--panel); border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  /* Set explicitly: .body .mono would otherwise win on specificity and shrink it. */
  .stat b { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 50px; line-height: .88; color: var(--ember);
    font-weight: 400; flex: none; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; min-width: 2.4ch; }
  .stat figcaption { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 14px; text-wrap: pretty; }

  .note { margin: 22px 0; padding: 15px 18px; border-left: 2px solid var(--ember);
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, #191410, transparent); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; }
  .note p { margin: 0; font-size: 14.5px; }
  .note.is-key { border-left-color: var(--green); background: linear-gradient(90deg, #121a15, transparent); }

  .cmp { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 22px 0; font-size: 13px; }
  .cmp th { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .07em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--dim); font-weight: 400; text-align: left; padding: 0 10px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .cmp td { padding: 8px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-soft); color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .cmp .num { text-align: right; color: var(--ink); }
  .cmp thead .num { text-align: right; color: var(--dim); }
  .cmp tr.edge td { background: #1a1512; color: var(--ink); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .doc { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 22px; }
    .toc { position: static; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 4px 14px;
      padding-bottom: 18px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
    .toc-h { width: 100%; margin-bottom: 4px; }
    .stat { flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
  }
</style>
